{
  "abstract": "The main theorem (thm:A, Theorem A \"Every dimension\", of version 1) of the manuscript \"Low-discrepancy quasi-uniform digital nets in every dimension\", in one statement. For every dimension s >= 3, every integer base q >= 2 all of whose prime factors are at least s - 1, and every k >= 1, the explicit digital net P^{U*_s}_{k,q} over Z/qZ with N = q^(sk) points (Definition def:Ns for the places infinity, 0, 1, ..., s-2 -- the Faure-Hammersley frame in graph form with the gauges of prop:Ns and prop:Gs -- and the design U*_s of Definition def:Ustar, whose columns are short vectors of the lattices of admissible columns, chosen level by level by Babai rounding) (1) is a (0,sk,s)-net in base q, so every cell of side q^(-k) holds exactly one point, and every point of [0,1]^s lies within Euclidean distance sqrt(s) N^(-1/s) of a net point (covering radius); (2) if q >= 5 a*_s + 4, any two distinct net points have toroidal and Euclidean distance at least (1 - a*_s/q) N^(-1/s), for every k >= 1, and for k >= 2 and q > (s-1) a*_s some two distinct points are closer than (1 - a*_s/q + (s-1)(a*_s+1)^2/q^2) N^(-1/s) (first-order sharpness of a*_s). The constant a*_s = max_c sum_{d != c} max_j |E_{d,j}[c]| of def:Ustar satisfies a*_3 = 2 and, for s >= 4, a*_s <= 2(s-1)^(3/2) (3/2)^(s-2) (s-2)!, and this explicit bound is e^(s log s + O(s)) (|log(bound) - s log s| <= 3s). So a*_s <= e^(s log s + O(s)), an upper bound only. Matching bounds of the same order N^(-1/s) for arbitrary N-point sets (the volume argument behind \"optimal order\") are classical and not formalized.\nComparator configuration: comparatorHeadline.json (4 compared theorems, all proved, no sorry): Z3NetA.theoremA, and three theorems showing inside the entry that the data the definitions introduce by choice are the paper's -- Z3NetA.ustarCols_spec (the columns E_{d,j} of def:Ustar exist and are unique), Z3NetA.gamma_spec (gamma_j of lem:sat(ii) exists and is unique) and Z3NetA.net_wellDefined (the reduction R_S -> Z/qZ, a solution U' of the residual problem and the coefficients beta of prop:Gs exist, and the net built from any of them is the entry's net). The Challenge (Headline/Challenge.lean) imports only Mathlib and contains verbatim copies of the 43 definitions of the proof side (Z3Net/FDefs.lean, Z3Net/ThmADefs.lean) that the statement reaches. The Solution (Headline/Solution.lean) obtains the theorem from Z3NetA.thmA of the full formalization in the same Lake project (whose single configuration comparator.json covers all compared theorems of the statement library).",
